                <pb facs="0278" n="250" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v05/pg250"/>

                <head>97. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On June 19, 1961, Admiral Burke, Chief of Naval Operations, forwarded to
                    Secretary of State Rusk a memorandum of the same date to the Joint Chiefs of
                    Staff in which he argued that Chairman Khrushchev may have “deliberately raised
                    the alarm on Berlin in order to seek concessions elsewhere. One of the most
                    dangerous areas for seeking such a concession would be Southeast Asia. He may
                    envision a relaxation of the Berlin crisis in exchange for an understanding that
                    the U.S. would not forcefully resist further Communist expansion in Southeast
                    Asia.” For text of Burkeʼs memorandum, see <ref
                        target="frus:frus1961-63v14/pg_129"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations,</hi> 1961–1963, volume XIV, pages 129</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus:frus1961-63v14/pg_130">130</ref>.</p>
            </div>
